            'notes' => [
                'purpose' => 'SQLite 3.35 or later. 3.35 is where the math functions can be built in, which ceil, floor, trunc, sign and pow all need (SQLITE_ENABLE_MATH_FUNCTIONS); a build without them refuses at run time rather than here, which is the one thing this document cannot check for you.',
                'two-directions' => 'This dialect is the map earning its keep in both directions. SQLite matches SEL more closely than MariaDB does in four places, so four things MariaDB needs a caveat or a refusal for are plain entries here: comparison is case-sensitive by default, upper() and lower() are ASCII-only exactly as SEL specifies, unicode() and char() are real code points, and length() counts code points. It matches SEL much less closely in one, and that one is arithmetic.',
                'decimal-float' => 'SQLite has no exact decimal type: arithmetic is int64 or IEEE double. That is not a scale difference, it is a different number system, so every arithmetic entry carries the caveat and `strict` refuses the lot. 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 here and 0.3 in SEL, and no template fixes that.',
                'numericLiteral' => 'A number is emitted as TEXT, which is the whole reason that lexical key exists. SQLite has no representation preserving 2.50\'s scale -- the literal 2.50 is a REAL and prints as 2.5 -- so `2.50 $== 2.5` would be TRUE here and FALSE in SEL. Quoted, SEL\'s exact characters survive, and SQLite\'s dynamic typing still reads them as a number wherever one is wanted: \'2.50\' + \'2.50\' is 5.0 just as 2.50 + 2.50 is.',
                'comparison-always-coerces' => 'The consequence of the above, and why every comparison entry gives `num` and `coerce` the same template. Two quoted numbers would otherwise compare as text, and SEL\'s == is numeric. CAST(\'2.50\' AS NUMERIC) = CAST(\'2.5\' AS NUMERIC) is 1, which is what SEL says.',
                'textCollate' => 'Empty, and not an omission. SQLite\'s default collation is BINARY, so \'A\' = \'a\' is already 0 -- what SEL says, and what MariaDB needed COLLATE utf8mb4_bin to be talked into. textCast is still needed: without it two numeric operands are compared as numbers.',
                'division' => 'The * 1.0 in the / template is not decoration. SQLite\'s / between two integers is INTEGER division -- 10 / 4 is 2 where SEL says 2.5 -- which is a different operation rather than an imprecise one. Forcing one operand to REAL makes it the same operation, inexactly, which is what the caveat then declares.',
                'modulo' => 'SQLite truncates both operands to integers before %: 5.5 % 2 is 1.0 where SEL says 1.5. That is exactly what the modulo-integer caveat names, so it is used rather than decimal-float.',
                'trim' => 'trim(X, Y) strips any character appearing in Y, so SEL\'s exact set -- space, tab, CR, LF -- is expressible directly. mysql-family needs REGEXP_REPLACE for the same thing.',
                'code-points' => 'CODE and CHAR are supported here and refused by mysql-family. SQLite\'s unicode() and char() are code points, which is what SEL means; MariaDB\'s ORD and CHAR are byte sequences, which is not.',
                'minmax' => 'min() and max() take one argument here, so no arity-keyed template is needed. LEAST and GREATEST do not, which is what the `*` fallback exists for.',
                'join' => 'Not overridden, so ansi\'s refusal stands. SQLite\'s group_concat specifies no order without an ORDER BY inside it, and a relation binding has no key to order by -- the same reason mysql-family refuses it.',
                'not-caveatable' => 'Two divergences cannot be expressed as a caveat because they are about failure rather than value. 1/0 is NULL here and E_DIV_ZERO in SEL, and CAST(\'abc\' AS NUMERIC) is 0 here where SEL raises E_NOT_NUM. A rule that would ERROR in SEL may quietly EVALUATE in SQLite. See §11 of docs/SQL-TRANSLATION.md.',
                'right' => 'substr(X, -N) alone is wrong for N = 0: -0 is 0, and substr(X, 0) behaves like substr(X, 1), so RIGHT("a", 0) answered "a" where SEL says "". The explicit length pins it. Found by the fuzz lane on sqlite\'s first run.',
                'comparison-is-exact-to-int64' => 'And why the six numeric comparisons carry decimal-float even though their templates look exact. CAST(text AS NUMERIC) yields an INTEGER while the value fits in int64 and a REAL once it does not, so comparison is exact through 19 digits and float at 20: CAST(\'99999999999999999999\' AS NUMERIC) = CAST(\'99999999999999999998\' AS NUMERIC) is 1, and SEL says FALSE. Probed digit by digit rather than reasoned about. The byte comparisons -- $==, $<, and the rest -- are separate entries with no cast and stay exact, and so does IN, which uses textCast. The cost is that the oracle stops failing the build on sqlite comparison; mysql-family and postgresql still do, which is the four-dialect corpus paying for the caveat.',
